  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Build a semantic-preserving lazy functional-DAG solver: cache each reached node’s selected successor and float tail cost, then reuse it across requested roots. Preserve the current strict-positive, first-on-tie neighbor choice, first matching ID behavior, unknown-root result, float addition order, root-token spelling/order, and `%f` output. Prefer iterative unwinding to avoid repeated recursive traversals; do not eagerly process unrelated nodes unless measurement justifies it.",
        "B": "Build an independent data-layout/I/O candidate: parse valid graph input once into dynamic compact adjacency storage and an ID-to-first-index lookup (without assuming dense IDs), resolving neighbor tokens to indices/sentinels. This removes repeated linear searches, copies, `strtok_r`, and `atoi` during traversal while retaining neighbor scan order and exact greedy semantics. Keep parsing robust for arbitrary valid line/list sizes and benchmark preprocessing cost against traversal savings.",
        "C": "Build a guarded four-core candidate around immutable preprocessed graph data: parallelize only independent row-successor preparation and/or independent requested-root evaluations, store results by query position, and emit serially in original order. Never use floating reductions or shared unsynchronized memo state. Use a generic workload threshold with a serial fallback, since the 11.804 ms baseline and its roughly 3% sample spread make thread overhead a material risk on this ARM64 4-core target.",
        "G": "Integrate only measured, correctness-validated pieces. First assess the serial B+A composition, then assess C separately and in composition; do not assume gains add. Require exact output on all cases, preserve the source-derived edge semantics and formatting, and retain the simplest faster variant. Treat improvements within timing noise skeptically; do not alter compiler, target, tests, oracle, budgets, or input-specific behavior.",
        "analysis": "The valid baseline averages 11.804 ms over three samples. Its hot path repeatedly linearly finds IDs and re-tokenizes neighbor strings for every recursive visit. A node’s chosen next hop depends only on immediate neighbor values, so valid DAG inputs permit cached successor-chain costs, but exact behavior requires strict `max < value` selection from an initial zero, original tie order, float—not double—accumulation, and unchanged query text/output formatting. With no GPU and only four cores, serial algorithm/data reductions are the primary opportunity; parallel work must amortize its overhead."
      },
